diff --git a/research/boot-chain-04/darwinboot/README.md b/research/boot-chain-04/darwinboot/README.md
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..ce2e4d9
--- /dev/null
+++ b/research/boot-chain-04/darwinboot/README.md
@@ -0,0 +1,51 @@
+# DarwinBoot
+
+This is an isolated K70 boot04 experiment, copied from the previously tested
+trace02 loader source. It is not the general DarwinBoot default. The matching
+profile exposes only the original vermeer DXE Heap (`BAB00000..C7CC0000`) for
+allocations. `AllocateWindow` accepts only a conventional-memory run inside
+that range and retains the kernel's 2 MiB virtual/physical phase. It allocates
+`EfiLoaderCode`; it does not disable EDK memory protection or fall back to
+other DRAM. The default remains 256 MiB, which cannot fit this heap and will
+be refused. The boot04 ADT must explicitly request 128 MiB.
+
+Before opening the payload or allocating kernel input buffers, the loader
+checks the actual `gSiliciumEntryArgHobGuid` FDT and its initrd bounds using a
+fixed stack memory map and bundled FdtLib. Missing, malformed, unreserved,
+unmapped or heap-overlapping inputs are rejected. This does not establish
+that earlier UEFI stages preserved those inputs. No physical storage driver
+or userdata write is added by this experiment.
+
+The kernel's VM span is 67,428,352 bytes. A 128 MiB window still reserves at
+least 2 MiB after all loaded data; failure is displayed before copying. The
+kernel file buffer is 51,171,328 bytes, so the file buffer plus 128 MiB window
+alone need 185,389,056 bytes (176.8 MiB), before EFI and filesystem allocations.
+A 209.75 MiB heap may therefore still run out of contiguous space. That result
+is a refusal, not evidence that the kernel entered or that memory elsewhere
+is safe. `Test/test-heap-window.py` and `Test/test-entry-inputs.py` exercise the
+real allocator/input guard with ASan/UBSan; a full firmware build and handset
+observation remain separate checks.
+
+UEFI XNU loader with packed RAM disk handoff support.
+
+The Mach-O parser accepts arm64 and arm64e MH_EXECUTE kernels, including
+the capability bits in the arm64e CPU subtype. XNU initializes pointer
+authentication and applies its own chained fixups. MH_FILESET input is
+still unsupported by this loader.
+
+UniXNU passes the selected boot entry's arguments to DarwinBoot, which copies
+them into XNU's command line and prints `DARWINBOOT: command line:` before
+handoff. The top green bar marks `DarwinEnter` before cache maintenance,
+MMU shutdown and the branch to XNU. Follow the
+[boot argument workflow](https://unixnu.com/xnu/boot-arguments#apply-arguments)
+and [green-bar diagnosis](https://unixnu.com/guide/debugging#green-bar).
+
+Build and interface details: [documentation](https://unixnu.com/reference/components).
+Sources and dependencies: [catalog](https://unixnu.com/reference/components).
+
+The optional `kernel.macho.vroot` sidecar carries up to 128 MiB of signed root
+policies. The loader checks its envelope and supplies its physical range as
+`/chosen/memory-map/RootPolicies`. XNU authenticates the policies using an owner
+anchor compiled into the source storage runtime. This handoff is independent
+of the Data RAM disk, GPU addon and Apple trust cache. The new handoff has not
+yet been built or exercised.
